{"content":"Perfect! I now have all the information needed to answer the question comprehensively.\n\n**q27.**\n\n**Mechanism:** Go's `embed` directive with Echo static middleware\n\n**Path:** `server/router/frontend/frontend.go`\n\n**How it works:**\n\nThe compiled React frontend is served in production through Go's built-in `embed` package (line 20-21 of `frontend.go`):\n\n```go\n//go:embed dist/*\nvar embeddedFiles embed.FS\n```\n\nThe build process works as follows:\n\n1. The React frontend is built with pnpm using the `release` script (defined in `web/package.json` line 11):\n   ```\n   \"release\": \"vite build --mode release --outDir=../server/router/frontend/dist --emptyOutDir\"\n   ```\n   This outputs the compiled frontend to `server/router/frontend/dist`\n\n2. The compiled files are embedded into the Go binary at build time using the `//go:embed dist/*` directive\n\n3. At runtime, the `FrontendService.Serve()` method (line 41-61 in `frontend.go`) registers Echo's `StaticMiddleware` to serve files from the embedded filesystem with proper cache headers and SPA fallback handling\n\nThis means the compiled frontend is bundled directly into the single Go binary with zero external file dependencies in production.","iteration":9,"turn":1,"type":"assistant"}
